    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Figure 12. Two ♯-moves convert T(9,2) (left) to the unknot (right)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 20,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 544.593, 540.022, 576.971 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1095",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "the standard projection. It follows that C(41)= 2. Clark also showed that C(K)≤ 2 ⋅ g(K)+ 1 for any knot K and asked if this upper bound was ever achieved."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 21,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 497.476, 540.025, 542.553 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1095",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Clark’s question was ﬁnally answered in 1995 by Murakami and Yasuhara [30] using the GordonLitherland pairing. To do this, they ﬁrst proved the following necessary condition for a knot K to bound a β1 = 2 nonorientable spanning surface F."
  }, {
    "type" : "list",
    "pdfua_tag" : "L",
    "id" : 22,
    "level" : "1",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 101.792, 539.995, 504.011 ],
    "numbering style" : "arabic numbers",
    "number of list items" : 2,
    "list items" : [ {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 471.633, 539.995, 504.011 ],
      "font" : "SFBX1095",
      "font size" : 10.909,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "Theorem 4.1 (Murakami-Yasuhara [30]). There is a pair of curves generating H1(F;Z) such that the Goeritz matrix of F takes the form:",
      "kids" : [ {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 1,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 262.92, 435.764, 314.804, 463.571 ],
        "font" : "MnSymbol10",
        "font size" : 10.909,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "GF(K)= "
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 2,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 314.761, 438.284, 343.899, 461.411 ],
        "font" : "CMMI10",
        "font size" : 10.909,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "l m m n"
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 3,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 72.001, 407.803, 467.466, 435.611 ],
        "font" : "SFTI1095",
        "font size" : 10.909,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "and satisﬁes σ(K)= sign(GF(K))−(l + 2m + n), where l,n are odd and m is even."
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 4,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 72.001, 334.723, 539.953, 414.371 ],
        "font" : "SFRM1095",
        "font size" : 10.909,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "Consider, as in [30], the knot 74. This knot has 3-genus 1 and hence C(74) ≤ 3. Since 74 is hyperbolic, we have C(74) ≥ 2. It therefore suﬃces to show that C(K) ≠ 2. Now, suppose that C(K) = 2. Since 74 has determinant 15, one can write out integral binary quadratic forms with determinant 15 (see e.g. Conway-Sloane [8]). Using a case analysis, it is then possible to show that no such binary quadratic form satisﬁes Theorem 4.1. Hence, it follows that C(74)= 3 = 2⋅g(74)+1."
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 5,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 72.001, 304.352, 539.972, 341.192 ],
        "font" : "SFRM1095",
        "font size" : 10.909,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "Crosscap numbers are in general diﬃcult to compute. Yet progress is currently being made. For example, Kalfagianni-Lee [19] determined upper and lower bounds on the crosscap numbers for several families of knots in terms of the coeﬃcients of the Jones polynomial."
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 6,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 72.001, 205.363, 540.022, 289.592 ],
        "font" : "SFRM1095",
        "font size" : 10.909,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "4.3. Non-orientable slice genus. The (smooth) 4-genus of a knot K is the smallest genus among all surfaces properly embedded in B4 such that ∂F = K. A well-known lower bound on the 4-genus is σ(K)  2. In analogy with crosscap number, the (smooth) non-orientable slice genus γ∗(K) of a knot K in S3 is deﬁned as the smallest ﬁrst Betti number of all compact connected non-orientable surfaces F smoothly embedded in B4 such that ∂F = K. If K is slice, then γ∗(K) is deﬁned to be 0. In 1975, Viro [39] showed that γ∗(41)= 2, and hence 41 does not bound a Möbius band in B4."
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 7,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 72.002, 123.032, 540.011, 220.21 ],
        "font" : "SFRM1095",
        "font size" : 10.909,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "Another proof that γ∗(41) = 2 follows from a result of Yasuhara, which is proved using the Gordon-Litherland pairing. Recall that the quadratic form q ∶ H1(F;Z 2Z) → Z 2Z of a knot K is deﬁned by q(x) = x⊺Ax, where F is a Seifert surface of K and A is a Seifert matrix for F. The Arf invariant of K is the Arf invariant of this quadratic form. Hence, Arf(K) = 0 if q maps a majority of elements of H1(F;Z 2Z) to 0 and otherwise Arf(K) = 1. The Arf invariant is also the (mod 2) coeﬃcient of z2 in the Conway polynomial of K. Note that there is a well-deﬁned injection Z 2Z → Z 8Z given by x → 4 ⋅ x."
      } ]
    }, {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 72.001, 101.792, 473.843, 114.163 ],
      "font" : "SFBX1095",
      "font size" : 10.909,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "Theorem 4.2 (Yasuhara [40]). If a knot K in S3 bounds a Möbius band in B4, then",
      "kids" : [ ]
    } ]
  }, {
